.IX Title "Koha::Regex::Replacement 3pm"
.TH Koha::Regex::Replacement 3pm "2026-10-29" "perl v5.32.1" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH "NAME"
Koha::Regex::Replacement \- expand a regular\-expression replacement string as data
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\&    use Koha::Regex::Replacement;
\&
\&    if ( $value =~ /$search/ ) {
\&        my $text = Koha::Regex::Replacement::expand_template(
\&            $replacement,      # the user\-supplied replacement string
\&            [ @{^CAPTURE} ],   # numbered capture groups from the match ($1 is element 0)
\&            { %+ },            # named capture groups from the match
\&        );
\&    }
.Ve
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
Koha lets staff run regular-expression find and replace over \s-1MARC\s0 fields, item
fields and call numbers. The replacement half of those substitutions used to be
run through Perl's \f(CW\*(C`s///ee\*(C'\fR, which evaluated the replacement as Perl code and
so allowed arbitrary code execution (see bug 30233).
.PP
Note: It was necessary to use \f(CW\*(C`s///ee\*(C'\fR because Perl does not provide a native
way of processing backreferences and backslash escapes in the replacement string
at runtime. While this doesn't matter when running hard-coded regular expressions,
it does make it difficult to support replacement strings provided by end users.
.PP
Note: While the \*(L"expand_template\*(R" may seem bespoke, it works similarly to
how Python's own template expansion works in Python's re.\fBsub()\fR regular
expression substitution function:
https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/3.15/Modules/_sre/sre.c#L3022
.PP
This module expands the replacement string as \fBdata\fR instead of evaluating it.
It walks the string once, from left to right, and only acts on the handful of
placeholders a replacement legitimately contains:
.IP "\(bu" 4
numbered backreferences: \f(CW$1\fR .. \f(CW$n\fR, \f(CW\*(C`${n}\*(C'\fR, and the older
        \f(CW\*(C`\e1\*(C'\fR .. \f(CW\*(C`\e9\*(C'\fR form
.IP "\(bu" 4
named captures: \f(CW$+{name}\fR and \f(CW\*(C`${name}\*(C'\fR
.IP "\(bu" 4
the escapes \f(CW\*(C`\en\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\er\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`\et\*(C'\fR, plus \f(CW\*(C`\e\e\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`\e$\*(C'\fR for a literal
        backslash or dollar sign
.PP
Every other character is copied through unchanged. Because the string is never
evaluated, interpolation blocks such as \f(CW\*(C`@{[ ... ]}\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`${ ... }\*(C'\fR end up in
the output as ordinary text rather than being run as Perl.
.PP
Case-folding escapes (\f(CW\*(C`\eU\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\eL\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\eu\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\el\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\eE\*(C'\fR) are deliberately left
out. They are a display nicety rather than a requirement, and omitting them
keeps this routine small and easy to follow. They can be added later if a real
call-number or \s-1MARC\s0 workflow turns out to need them.
.SH "FUNCTIONS"
.IX Header "FUNCTIONS"
.SS "expand_template"
.IX Subsection "expand_template"
.Vb 1
\&    my $text = Koha::Regex::Replacement::expand_template( $replacement, \e@captures, \e%named_captures );
.Ve
.PP
Returns \f(CW$replacement\fR with the placeholders listed above resolved. Numbered
backreferences are looked up in \f(CW\*(C`\e@captures\*(C'\fR (element 0 is group 1, matching
\&\f(CW\*(C`@{^CAPTURE}\*(C'\fR) and named captures in \f(CW\*(C`\e%named_captures\*(C'\fR (matching \f(CW\*(C`%+\*(C'\fR). A
reference to a group that did not match, or to a name that is not present,
expands to the empty string. The input is treated purely as data and is never
run as code.
